---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { useTranslations } from '../i18n';
import { repoFile, url } from '../lib/url.ts';
import { SITE } from '../site.config.ts';

const t = useTranslations();
---

<Base title={t('about.title')} description={t('about.description')}>
  <article class="lesson mx-auto max-w-[720px] py-8 sm:py-12">
    <h1>{t('about.title')}</h1>
    <p>
      Xtraplaytion is a playground for fitting curves. Pick a function, choose where the data lives,
      and watch neural networks and classical models learn it, live in your browser. Then see what
      they do in the gaps and beyond the data, where they have to guess.
    </p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Every model trains on your own device, in background threads of your browser. There is no
        server doing the work, and none that receives your experiments.
      </li>
      <li>
        On the processor, training uses its own math library, so the same experiment gives exactly
        the same numbers in every browser. That is why a shared link rebuilds the same race.
      </li>
      <li>
        On the graphics card (through WebGPU), training is much faster but approximate: graphics
        cards compute with fewer digits, so results can differ a little from device to device.
      </li>
      <li>
        A few heavy races come as recordings made on a graphics card, marked Recorded, so they play
        on any device. You can always train them yourself.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Xtraplaytion has no server, no database and no accounts. It is free, with no ads and no
      subscription.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Your experiments stay in your browser. A share link keeps the whole experiment after the #
        sign of the address, the part that browsers never send to a server. The library keeps your
        saves in this browser only.
      </li>
      <li>
        Your profile (a name and a picture, both optional) is kept in this browser and inside your
        save file. A photo you pick is cut to a small square on your device and is never uploaded,
        except to your own cloud save if you turn it on.
      </li>
      <li>
        The save file holds your profile and your saved experiments. It is how you sign in on
        another device: download it, then open it there, or keep it in your cloud.
      </li>
      <li>
        One cookie, and only if you agree: if you accept in the cookie bar, Google Analytics counts
        visits. It gets the address of the page without the part after the # sign or the ? sign, and
        nothing you made, nor your name or picture. If you decline, it does not load at all.
      </li>
      <li>Races embedded in other sites never load analytics.</li>
    </ul>
    {SITE.analytics.enabled && (
      <p>
        <button type="button" class="xp-btn min-h-11" data-consent-open>
          {t('footer.cookies')}
        </button>
      </p>
    )}

    <h3 id="cloud-save">Cloud save</h3>
    <p>
      Cloud save is off unless you turn it on in <a href={url('user/')}>your profile</a>. When you
      connect Google Drive or Dropbox, your browser writes your save file straight to your own
      account and reads it back from there, so your profile and experiments follow you to your other
      devices. Nothing passes through a server of ours.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Google Drive:</strong> Xtraplaytion asks only for its own hidden app folder in your
        Drive (the permission called drive.appdata). It cannot see, open or change any other file in
        your Drive. It also reads the name and email address of your Google account, only to show
        which account is connected.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dropbox:</strong> Xtraplaytion uses its own app folder, Apps/Xtraplaytion, and reads
        the name and email address of your Dropbox account to show which account is connected.
      </li>
      <li>
        The keys that Google or Dropbox hand out for this, and the account name and email, are kept
        in this browser only. Google's sign-in script loads only when you connect or save to Google
        Drive.
      </li>
      <li>
        You can disconnect or sign out at any time, which also withdraws the access. Your save file
        stays in your cloud until you delete it: in Google Drive under Settings, Manage apps,
        Xtraplaytion, Delete hidden app data; in Dropbox in the Apps/Xtraplaytion folder. You can
        also remove the access from your Google Account (Security, third-party connections) or in
        Dropbox (Settings, Connected apps).
      </li>
      <li>
        Data from your cloud is used only to keep your save. It is never sold, shared, used for ads
        or used to train AI models, and nobody but you can read it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Xtraplaytion's use and transfer of information received from Google APIs adheres to the{' '}
      <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">
        Google API Services User Data Policy
      </a>
      , including the Limited Use requirements. Questions about privacy are welcome on the project's{' '}
      <a href={`${SITE.repoUrl}/issues`}>GitHub issues</a> page.
    </p>

    <h2>What you make is yours</h2>
    <p>
      Images, videos, GIFs, files and saved experiments you create here belong to you. Use and share
      them however you like, with or without credit. Exported videos carry a small Made with
      Xtraplaytion line that you can switch off. Fonts inside them keep their own open licenses.
    </p>

    <h2>Embedding</h2>
    <p>
      You may put a race from this site on your own page: open the playground, press Share, then
      Embed on a page, and paste the code. The race keeps pointing to this site and trains in each
      visitor's browser. Please do not present Xtraplaytion as your own work.
    </p>

    <h2>License</h2>
    <p>
      Xtraplaytion is source-available, not open source. The source code is public under the{' '}
      <a href={repoFile('LICENSE')}>KFA Source-Available License 1.0</a>. The components it builds
      on and their licenses are listed in the{' '}
      <a href={url('THIRD_PARTY.md')}>third-party notices</a>. The source lives on{' '}
      <a href={SITE.repoUrl}>GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
